Question #8:

In the vectors-recycling chunk, z has 6 elements and short has only 2. R still produced an answer instead of an error.

Explain, in your own words, what R’s “recycling rule” did to short to make the addition work.

ANSWER: R repeated short until it matched the length of z, so c(1, 0) became c(1, 0, 1, 0, 1, 0) before the addition.



Question #9: Now try adding z (length 6) to c(1, 0, 1) (length 3) instead. Does it work cleanly?

ANSWER:Yes, it works cleanly with no warning. c(1, 0, 1) is recycled exactly twice to c(1, 0, 1, 1, 0, 1), because 6 is a multiple of 3.


Then try adding z to c(1, 0, 1, 1) (length 4). Compare the warning (or lack of one) in each case, and explain what condition determines whether R recycles silently versus with a warning.

ANSWER: The length 3 case gives no warning, while the length 4 case gives a warning. c(1, 0, 1, 1) is recycled to c(1, 0, 1, 1, 1, 0), so it is cut off partway through its second pass. R recycles silently only when the length of the longer vector is an exact multiple of the shorter one. Otherwise it still computes a result but warns you.

