Question #12

Explain, step by step, why inventory$item[inventory$in_stock] works even though item and in_stock are separate list elements.

ANSWER:

inventory$in_stock extracts the logical vector c(TRUE, TRUE, FALSE).
inventory$item extracts the character vector c("Notebook", "Pen", "Eraser").
[ ] keeps the elements of item where the logical vector is TRUE, matching by position.

It works because both vectors have the same length and the same order, so position 1 in each refers to the same product.

Question #13

Suppose a classmate stores related data as three separate vectors instead of one list. What risks could this create? Argue for or against using a list (or a data frame).

ANSWER: Separate vectors can easily fall out of sync. If you sort, delete, or add to one vector but forget the others, prices and stock status end up attached to the wrong items, and R won’t warn you. A list (or better, a data frame) keeps related data together in one object, so it’s easier to pass around and less error-prone. I’d argue for using a list or data frame.

Conclusion

ANSWER: Vectors hold many values of a single type, while lists can hold different types and structures, including vectors and other lists. Vectors are indexed with [ ], while lists need [ ] for a sub-list and [[ ]] or $ for the element itself. I would use a vector for a simple series of same-type values (like a set of scores) and a list when grouping related but different kinds of data (like a student record). For tabular data where every column has the same length, a data frame is the better choice.